  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Saturday)\nIssued at 1146 AM MDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nRain showers have begun to push into the region this morning as \nupper low over the southwestern US moves northeastward. Large \nscale Q-vector convergence and moisture advection will continue to\nsupport this precip through the overnight hours into Monday. In \nterms of what kind of precip is going to fall, the majority will \nbe rain. However, as the colder air associated with the upper low \npushes into the region, we could see a rain/snow mix on portions \nof the western SD plains and a transition to snow over the Black \nHills by late Monday morning. Snow will likely be falling during \nthe afternoon hours when sfc temps will be in the mid 30s and even\nthough the thermal profiles will support snow, they'll still be a\ntad warm. All of this means that we likely won't see much aside \nfrom some minor, slushy accumulations (generally around a trace to\nmaybe an inch) that should melt quickly on the warm ground.\n\nUpper level shortwave and attendant moisture starved front will \nmove in from the PNW later on Tuesday. Could see some light rain \nshowers across far northeastern WY into northwestern SD with this \nsystem with the only other impacts expected being breezy southwest\nwinds across the western SD plains and portions of northeastern \nWY late Tuesday afternoon. Otherwise, upper level west-\nsouthwesterly flow will keep temperatures seasonably mild after \nTuesday with dry weather expected through the late week.",
